<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="
          http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
          https://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.23.xsd">

    <!--
      2026-09-22: analytics must see EVERY finished game with a real person in
      it, not only the ones that count for the competitive record.

      Until now the Node reporter dropped an ineligible game (§8.1: both teams
      need a human) before it ever reached the backend. The consequence the
      owner saw on the dashboard: a person who played with three bots, or
      against the demo lobby's fake people, produced no row anywhere — "Tko je
      igrao" showed 0 players on a site people had actually played on. And a
      guest was stored with no identifier at all, so guests could be counted
      but never listed.

      Two additions, no semantic change to anything already stored:

        * game_results.eligible — §8.1's verdict, now CARRIED instead of acted
          on. Every competitive read (profile stats, karma window) filters on
          it, so the personal record keeps exactly the meaning it had; the
          admin analytics reads without the filter and finally sees everything.
        * game_result_players.player_name / player_kind — the seat's display
          name as shown at the table, and what occupied it. `player_name` is
          the ONLY handle a guest ever has (they have no uid by design), so it
          is what makes a guest listable rather than merely countable.

      Backfill of existing rows:
        * eligible = true — every row already in the table arrived through the
          old reporter, which only sent eligible games. Marking them true is
          not a guess, it is the old invariant written down.
        * player_kind = 'BOT' when is_bot, else 'PLAYER' when uid is present,
          else 'GUEST' (a uid-less human seat is a guest — the write path
          rejects any other combination).
        * player_name stays NULL for old rows: nobody recorded it and it
          cannot be reconstructed. Old guest seats therefore keep appearing
          only in the aggregate guest counters, never as named rows.

      Both columns are NULLABLE with a DEFAULT rather than NOT NULL: an older
      game server that has not been redeployed yet keeps posting the old body,
      and it must keep working (the intake derives `eligible` from the old
      rule when the field is absent).

      Index: game_result_players(player_name) — the admin list groups guests
      by the name they played under, and that grouping must not seq-scan the
      whole table as it grows. A plain index rather than a partial one on
      `uid IS NULL`: Liquibase's <createIndex> has no portable WHERE, and the
      saving would be small anyway since most rows carry a name.

      columnExists / MARK_RAN so a database where these were added by hand is
      a no-op rather than a startup failure.
    -->

    <changeSet id="2026-09-22-game-results-analytics" author="mrodek">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <columnExists tableName="game_results" columnName="eligible"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>

        <addColumn tableName="game_results">
            <!-- §8.1's verdict. Default true so an older reporter, which only
                 ever sent eligible games, keeps producing correct rows. -->
            <column name="eligible" type="boolean" defaultValueBoolean="true"/>
        </addColumn>

        <addColumn tableName="game_result_players">
            <!-- Display name at the table. NULL for rows written before this
                 changeset; the reporter always sends one now. -->
            <column name="player_name" type="varchar(64)"/>
            <column name="player_kind" type="varchar(8)"/>
        </addColumn>

        <sql>
            UPDATE game_results SET eligible = TRUE WHERE eligible IS NULL;

            UPDATE game_result_players
               SET player_kind = CASE
                       WHEN is_bot THEN 'BOT'
                       WHEN uid IS NOT NULL THEN 'PLAYER'
                       ELSE 'GUEST'
                   END
             WHERE player_kind IS NULL;

            ALTER TABLE game_result_players
                ADD CONSTRAINT ck_game_result_players_kind
                    CHECK (player_kind IS NULL
                           OR player_kind IN ('PLAYER', 'GUEST', 'BOT', 'DEMO'));
        </sql>

        <createIndex tableName="game_result_players"
                     indexName="idx_game_result_players_guest_name">
            <column name="player_name"/>
        </createIndex>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
